✅ List vs ArrayList in Java

| Feature                  | `List`                                      | `ArrayList`                                                    |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Type                     | Interface                                   | Concrete class                                                 |
| Instantiable?            | ❌ No (`new List<>()` fails)                 | ✅ Yes                                                          |
| Extends / Implements     | Extends `Collection`                        | Implements `List`, `RandomAccess`, `Cloneable`, `Serializable` |
| Role                     | Defines the contract (methods)              | Array-backed implementation of that contract                   |
| Performance              | Depends on the concrete class               | Fast random access `O(1)` get; slower mid insert/delete        |
| Typical declaration      | `List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();`    | `ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();`                  |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Example

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Preferred: program to the List interface
        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
        names.add("Ali");
        names.add("Sara");

        System.out.println(names); // [Ali, Sara]
    }
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Why declare as `List` instead of `ArrayList`?

```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
// later, if needed:
names = new LinkedList<>();
```

- Call sites / method params stay the same (`List` API).
- You can switch implementation without rewriting client code.
- Caveat: only works if you didn’t use `ArrayList`-only methods (`ensureCapacity`, `trimToSize`) or cast to `ArrayList`.

🎤 Interview answer (say this):
We declare it as `List` and create it with `ArrayList` because of **programming to an interface**.

`List` is the contract — it only exposes what a list can do (`add`, `get`, `remove`, etc.).
`ArrayList` is just one implementation of that contract.

So if later we need a different implementation, we can change only the creation side — the rest of the code still works because it depends on `List`,
not on `ArrayList`-specific methods.

One-liner: “We use `List` for flexibility — code depends on the interface, so we can switch from `ArrayList` to `LinkedList` (or another `List`)
without changing the rest of the code.”

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary

| Type        | Meaning                                              |
| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `List`      | Interface for ordered, indexable collections         |
| `ArrayList` | Resizable-array implementation of `List`             |

Rule of thumb: declare as `List`, create with `ArrayList` (unless you need LinkedList / another impl).

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) `List` cannot be instantiated — always need a concrete class (`ArrayList`, `LinkedList`, …).
2) “Flexible” means programming to the interface — swapping impl still changes performance (e.g. LinkedList `get(i)` is `O(n)`).
3) Prefer `List` for fields/params/return types; use `ArrayList` only when you need its specific methods.
4) `ArrayList` is **not** thread-safe.
5) `Vector` also implements `List` but is legacy/synchronized — prefer `ArrayList` + external sync / concurrent collections.
